<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Media Atropatene</h1><p class="st-meta">323 BCE – 226 CE (approx.) · capital Ganzak</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Hellenistic Iranian kingdom in north-western Iran founded by the Persian satrap Atropates, whose name survives in 'Azerbaijan'.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atropates">Atropates</a> had served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/media-region">Media</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. After Alexander's death in 323 BCE, while the rest of Media passed to his successors, Atropates kept the northern part and ruled it as an independent kingdom, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called Media Atropatene.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganzak">Ganzak</a>, near the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">fire temple</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takht-e-soleyman">Takht-e Soleyman</a>, over a land of horse pastures and mountains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> was strong, and the region later held one of the great fire temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adur-gushnasp">Adur Gushnasp</a>. The kingdom stood between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>: in 36 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artavasdes-i-of-media-atropatene">Artavasdes I</a> helped repulse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony's</a> siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phraaspa">Phraaspa</a>, then allied with him, and from the 1st century CE members of the Arsacid house often held the throne.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Atropatene came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> around 226 CE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> conquered it and made it a province. Its name became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> Aturpatakan and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/media-atropatene.jpg" alt="Media Atropatene" width="320" height="233"><figcaption>Image: Golden · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atropatene_as_a_vassal_of_Seleucids.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>323 BCE – 226 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ganzak</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Atropates, Artavasdes I, Ariobarzanes II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Median, Parthian, Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iran</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Media Atropatene, Atropatene, Atropatkan</dd></dl></aside></div>
